fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "Domes as Honoring the Pattern",
    "body": "Gardner traces his dome obsession to South Florida hurricanes, a father in commercial construction who kept telling him square boxes do not handle wind, and a 60 Minutes segment about a resilient dome in North Carolina. After a brutal rainy season and earthquake season in Costa Rica turned well built cubes into parallelograms, he trained at Cal Earth University, then evolved toward the Japanese star dome, which he says holds the whole structure under tension like a bridge. His largest permanent build ran 42 feet across, roughly 1,600 round feet, with a center 27 feet high."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Egg Shape and Centropy",
    "body": "Reading Callum Coats on Victor Schauberger sent Gardner toward the egg, whose hemispherical base he calls female and whose lancet arch he calls male; joined, he argues, they produce a child and a continuous flow that Eileen McKusick named centropy rather than negentropy. He tested it by capping a lancet arch beehive dome with a hemispherical shell, and reports that a visiting shaman felt a third, youthful energy afterward, and that pregnant women described being pulled clockwise inside one shape and planted into the ground inside the other. Chance Garton ties the same principle to bass traps and harsh square studios."
  },
  {
    "title": "Elementals Gathering Comes Together",
    "body": "The gathering runs May 8 to 10 on the Beritaria Festival campgrounds in south middle Missouri, with Garton hosting as MC and giving a talk of his own. Gardner says the idea came from the Bigelsen brothers Terrainology conference in New York, where a producer told him he needed his own event; when he called around, only two people declined because of prior commitments. The lineup he names includes Tom Barnett, Dr. Marizelle Arce, Amy Hamilton, Emily Moyer, Kyle Denton of Tipi Canoe Herbs, Steve Young and Owen Benjamin, with details posted at elementalsgathering.com."
  },
  {
    "title": "Earth Work and Becoming Ground",
    "body": "Gardner says bodywork in South Florida was making him sick until he moved to Central America and started building rocket mass heaters out of cob, trudging for hours through mud, straw, sand and lime; he could feel the pH in his mouth shift in the pit, and afterward he stopped getting sick from clients. Retreats eventually hired him simply to sit in the corner and be the ground for the room. For winter he points to Epsom salt baths, describing tub, porcelain and salt as clay, metal and electrolyte, a theme he expands in his Substack piece Sarcophagi."
  },
  {
    "title": "Black Gold Biochar Launches",
    "body": "Gardner launched Black Gold Biochar in November, selling a five gallon bag for $35, enough he says to establish a 100 square foot bed. He distinguishes biochar from charcoal by charge, arguing biochar carries a net negative charge and is the most diamagnetic substance on earth, so it attracts positively charged toxins the way hospital activated carbon does. He cites Dr. Marizelle Arce's microscopy of his char, ash eaten with corn in Central America for niacin, burnt toast in industrial England, and Sabrina Wallace and Eric Dollard on signals traveling through the ground rather than the wires."
  }
]

